    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im studying for my security+ exam and i'm a bit confused. i get that hashing turns a password into a string of characters, but why do we actually need to add a 'salt' to it? like if the hash is already unique, why does the extra step matter?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The main reason to use a salt is to defend against rainbow table attacks. Without a salt, identical passwords hash to the same value, allowing attackers to use pre-computed tables of hashes. By adding a unique, random string (the salt) to each password before hashing, even identical passwords will produce completely different hash outputs, thus invalidating pre-computed tables. This forces an attacker to brute-force each entry individually.",
      "source": "target",
