    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if i use the same salt for every single user in my database, does that still count as salting or is it basically useless? i'm trying to figure out if the salt needs to be unique per user or something else.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Using the same salt for every user defeats the primary purpose of salting; it only slows down simple pre-computation, but is easily bypassed. For effective security, the salt **must** be unique and randomly generated for each individual user account. This ensures that if an attacker obtains the hashed passwords, they must compute a separate hash for every single entry. Therefore, uniqueness per user is critical for robust password security.",
